From: djnice <djnice@freemail.hu>
Subject: usb: core: let the device tree disable autosuspend

The Dune HD Pro One 8K Plus has a VIA VL817 hub on its USB 3.0 ports. Once
runtime suspended, its remote wakeup never reaches the xHCI, so devices
plugged later, or a boot disk that comes up late, are never seen and the
box cannot boot from those ports. usbcore.autosuspend=-1 fixes it; allow
the board device tree to ask for the same with "coreelec,usb-no-autosuspend"
in its root node.
---
--- a/drivers/usb/core/usb.c	2026-09-30 20:09:20.602077121 +0200
+++ b/drivers/usb/core/usb.c	2026-09-30 20:09:20.619359886 +0200
@@ -33,6 +33,7 @@
 #include <linux/spinlock.h>
 #include <linux/errno.h>
 #include <linux/usb.h>
+#include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/usb/hcd.h>
 #include <linux/mutex.h>
 #include <linux/workqueue.h>
@@ -1131,6 +1132,17 @@
 		pr_info("%s: USB support disabled\n", usbcore_name);
 		return 0;
 	}
+#ifdef CONFIG_PM
+	/*
+	 * Some boards have a USB 3.0 hub whose remote wakeup never reaches the
+	 * host controller: once suspended, it misses every new device. Let the
+	 * device tree keep USB devices awake, like usbcore.autosuspend=-1.
+	 */
+	if (of_property_read_bool(of_root, "coreelec,usb-no-autosuspend")) {
+		pr_info("%s: autosuspend disabled by the device tree\n", usbcore_name);
+		usb_autosuspend_delay = -1;
+	}
+#endif
 	usb_init_pool_max();
 
 	usb_debugfs_init();
